items.test:
New file apps/mobile/src/screens/game/hit/__tests__/warpStart.test.tsx, 6 tests. I ran it against the snap-loop12 HitLayer and hitClock: the 4 tests marked 'fails on old code' failed there.

(a) [fails on old code] HitLayer with standby='warp' and no hit: the 'hit-warp' canvas is mounted in a region at opacity 0, with no banner and no 'hit-layer' or 'hit-layer-region'. When the warp comes, the same canvas is used (a mount-counting Skia mock still shows 1 mount), the region is shown and the banner is there. After the end it is unseen again and still mounted.
(b) Without standby, nothing is mounted between hits.
(c) [fails on old code] A hit received 180 ms into its time starts at 0. The old code gave 180.
(d) [fails on old code] A freeze while the hit is on keeps the drawn value. The old code re-based to the server's value.
(e) A hit that arrives frozen is drawn where it stands.
(f) [fails on old code] hitClockStep, with a 200 ms mount frame and then 40 ms frames:
- every step during the entrance is ≤ 34 ms of display time, scaled to the catch-up pace;
- the first frame's alpha is < 0.2;
- the fade-in spans at least 6 frames;
- the clock lands on 5000 exactly at the end and not before.

Existing tests updated to the new intended behaviour:
- BoardHitLayer.test.tsx: a fresh mount 120 ms or 100 ms into the hit (normal and reduced) now starts its fade from 0.
- effects.test.tsx: the 600 ms tilt case now ends the earlier hit first, because a moved deadline on the same hit continues.

Green:
- 93 suites that mention HitLayer, interference, GameScreen, hitClock or worklet: 2310 tests, including GameScreenDuel and interferenceRanked.e2e;
- the motion.md docs suites, including reducedMotion and levelsSpecDocs;
- npx tsc -p apps/mobile --noEmit;
- eslint on the changed files.
All changed files are LF.

items.rootCause:
The loop-12 measurements point to two causes.

(1) The hit clock was read from the server. On the receiver the clock is server-synced, so by the time the phone commits the hit it is already about 100–200 ms in: the time from the sender's tap, through the server, to this phone. The mount frame then took another 150–230 ms by framestats, so the first frame anyone saw was already past the 240 ms fade-in. Each part (the RN dim, the RN banner, the Skia overlay) came in at full strength in a single frame, at whatever moment it reached the screen. This matches 'full strength in one frame' in every run, and the partial 2-step fade of the second warp, which had lower latency.

(2) A new Skia surface at every warp. HitLayer returned null between hits, so every warp mounted a new non-opaque WarpOverlay <Canvas>, which RN Skia 2.6.2 backs with a new SkiaTextureView. Its picture shows only after onSurfaceTextureAvailable, the GL window, a Skia draw and an HWUI latch, 1–4 frames after the plain-view banner and dim that mount in the same commit. That is why the overlay was always last, 94–348 ms after TSA. It is also why HitRehearsal did not help: it makes and drops canvases of its own. The 'updateAndRelease() failed' lines are harmless.

Ruled out:
- the sound: with sound off the split and the stall are the same size;
- GC;
- separate clocks: there is already one.

Not fixed here, because it is native/Reanimated cost and outside this item's evidence:
- the cost of running the effect (15–48 ms UI anim and 12–36 ms RenderThread per frame);
- the mount-frame cost.

